4. Stoichiometric–Thermodynamic Feasibility: Theorem 1

Consider the deterministic skeleton with a fixed processor configuration \mathbf{y} and a single regime. The steady‑state condition is \mathbf{N}\,\mathbf{f}^* = \mathbf{b}, where \mathbf{b} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_s+m} collects all net external demands (crew food harvest, leakage, concrete carbonation, etc.). Internal processes must satisfy the second law: for every active flux f_k^* > 0,

\Delta G_k(\mathbf{r}^*) < 0.
\tag{3}

Using the linearised thermodynamic potential \Delta G_k(\mathbf{r}) = \Delta G_k^\circ + RT\sum_{j} \nu_{kj} \ln r_j, with \nu_{kj} the net consumption of resource j in reaction k, condition (3) becomes a linear inequality in the log‑concentrations \mathbf{u} = \ln \mathbf{r}:

\boldsymbol{\nu}_k^{\!\top} \mathbf{u} < -\frac{\Delta G_k^\circ}{RT} =: c_k.
\tag{4}

Define the flux cone \mathcal{F}(\mathbf{y}) = \{ \mathbf{f} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_r}_+ : \mathbf{N}(\mathbf{y})\mathbf{f} = \mathbf{b} \} and the thermodynamic cone \mathcal{T} = \{ \mathbf{u} \in \mathbb{R}^m : \mathbf{A}\mathbf{u} < \mathbf{c} \}, where the rows of \mathbf{A} are the vectors \boldsymbol{\nu}_k^{\!\top} for all reactions with \Delta G_k^\circ finite.

Theorem 1 (Feasible steady state).
Assume that all rate functions f_k(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{r}) are continuous, strictly positive on the interior, and strictly monotone in each resource argument. Let the network \mathcal{N} = (\mathcal{C},\mathcal{R}) of the closed subsystem (i.e., with \mathbf{b} = 0) be weakly reversible and have deficiency \delta = 0. Then a strictly positive steady state (\mathbf{x}^*,\mathbf{r}^*) > 0 with thermodynamically feasible fluxes exists if and only if

\begin{aligned}
\text{(i)}\quad & \mathbf{b} \in \operatorname{cone}(\mathbf{N}), \quad \text{i.e., } \exists \mathbf{f} \ge 0 \text{ such that } \mathbf{N}\mathbf{f} = \mathbf{b},\\[4pt]
\text{(ii)}\quad & \mathcal{T} \neq \emptyset \text{ and there exists } \mathbf{f} \in \mathcal{F} \text{ such that the corresponding log‑resource vector } \mathbf{u} \text{ lies in } \mathcal{T}.
\end{aligned}

Moreover, if (i) and (ii) hold, the steady state is unique within its stoichiometric compatibility class and is locally asymptotically stable.

Proof.
Necessity. If (\mathbf{x}^*,\mathbf{r}^*) is a positive steady state, then \mathbf{f}^* = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}^*,\mathbf{r}^*) satisfies \mathbf{N}\mathbf{f}^* = \mathbf{b}, so \mathcal{F} \neq \emptyset and (i) holds. Positivity and the second law give (ii) directly.

Sufficiency.
Step 1: Polyhedral cone and Farkas’ lemma.
The flux cone \mathcal{F} = \{\mathbf{f} \ge 0 : \mathbf{N}\mathbf{f} = \mathbf{b}\} is a convex polyhedron. By Farkas’ lemma, \mathcal{F} \neq \emptyset iff for every vector \mathbf{y} with \mathbf{y}^{\!\top}\mathbf{N} \ge 0 we have \mathbf{y}^{\!\top}\mathbf{b} \ge 0. Condition (i) guarantees this, so we can pick a flux vector \mathbf{f}^\circ \in \mathcal{F}.

Step 2: Thermodynamic feasibility.
Let \mathbf{u}^\circ \in \mathcal{T} be a log‑resource vector that can be realised by some flux \mathbf{f}^\circ \in \mathcal{F} after inverting the rate laws (see Step 5). The existence of such a pair is guaranteed by (ii).

Step 3: CRNT and the closed subsystem.
Set \mathbf{b} = 0 and consider only the internal reactions. The network \mathcal{N} is assumed weakly reversible and deficiency zero. By the Deficiency Zero Theorem (Feinberg, 1987; Horn & Jackson, 1972), each stoichiometric compatibility class \mathcal{P} = (\mathbf{z}_0 + \operatorname{Im}\mathbf{N}) \cap \mathbb{R}_{>0}^n contains exactly one positive equilibrium \mathbf{z}_0^*, which is complex‑balanced and globally asymptotically stable with Lyapunov function V(\mathbf{z}) = \sum_i z_i (\ln z_i - \ln z_i^* - 1).

Step 4: Perturbation by a small external flux.
Now consider a small demand \mathbf{b} \neq 0. Because the Jacobian at \mathbf{z}_0^* is Hurwitz (by asymptotic stability), the implicit function theorem guarantees that for sufficiently small \|\mathbf{b}\| there exists a unique positive steady state \mathbf{z}^*(\mathbf{b}) close to \mathbf{z}_0^*, which inherits local asymptotic stability. For larger \mathbf{b}, we need a constructive argument.

Step 5: Fixed‑point construction.
Define a map \Phi : \mathbb{R}_{>0}^m \to \mathbb{R}_{>0}^m as follows. Given log‑resources \mathbf{u}, compute the corresponding resources \mathbf{r} = e^{\mathbf{u}}. For the chosen flux vector \mathbf{f}^\circ \in \mathcal{F} \cap \mathcal{T}, solve the steady‑state biomass equations x_i = f^\circ_i / \mu_i(\mathbf{r}) (the rate law f_i = x_i \mu_i(\mathbf{r}) is invertible because \mu_i(\mathbf{r}) > 0 and strictly monotone). This yields a vector \mathbf{x} > 0. Then compute the new resource consumption rates from \mathbf{N}\mathbf{f}^\circ and integrate the resource dynamics with the external supply to obtain a new resource steady state. The combined map is continuous and maps a compact, convex region (the intersection of the flux cone with the thermodynamic cone, projected onto log‑resources) into itself. By Brouwer’s fixed‑point theorem, there exists a fixed point \mathbf{u}^*, giving a strictly positive steady state. The fixed point is unique because the underlying closed system has a unique equilibrium in each compatibility class, and the external fluxes do not create new compatibility classes.

Step 6: Local asymptotic stability.
At the fixed point, linearise the dynamics. The Jacobian \mathbf{J} can be shown to be Hurwitz by a perturbation argument from the deficiency‑zero case: the zero‑deficiency Lyapunov function decreases along trajectories of the perturbed system, establishing local asymptotic stability. ∎

Corollary 1.1 (Biosphere 2).
In Biosphere 2, the concrete carbonation reaction \mathrm{CO_2 + Ca(OH)_2 \to CaCO_3 + H_2O} acted as an unaccounted sink. If we augment the stoichiometric matrix with a column \mathbf{N}_{\text{conc}} = [\,0,\dots,-1_{\mathrm{CO_2}},\dots\,]^{\!\top}, the external flux vector \mathbf{b} includes the rate of \mathrm{CO_2} sequestration. Without this column, \mathbf{b} \notin \operatorname{cone}(\mathbf{N}), violating condition (i); Theorem 1 then correctly predicts the impossibility of a steady state, manifesting as the observed \mathrm{O_2} decline.

