5. Uniform Persistence and Stochastic Permanence: Theorem 2

5.1 Deterministic persistence under Liebig’s law

We now study the closed ecosystem with no external demands (\mathbf{b}=0) and Liebig–Monod kinetics (2). The dynamics are

\begin{aligned}
\dot{x}_i &= x_i\big( \mu_i(\mathbf{r}) - D_i \big), \quad i=1,\dots,n_s,\\
\dot{r}_j &= \underbrace{\text{recycling}_j(\mathbf{x})}_{\text{from waste, mortality, etc.}} - \sum_{i: j \in \mathcal{R}_i} c_{ji}\, x_i\, \mu_i(\mathbf{r}),
\end{aligned}
\tag{5}

where D_i are death/dilution rates and c_{ji} are stoichiometric coefficients. We assume the system is dissipative, i.e., total mass \sum_i x_i + \sum_j r_j is bounded.

Theorem 2 (Deterministic persistence).
Assume a strictly positive steady state \mathbf{z}^* > 0 exists (guaranteed by Theorem 1). Let E_{\mathcal{J}} denote a boundary equilibrium where a subset of guilds \mathcal{J} \subsetneq \{1,\dots,n_s\} is extinct. The system (5) is uniformly persistent if and only if for every such boundary equilibrium, there exists a missing guild i \notin \mathcal{J} with a positive invasion growth rate

\lambda_i(E_{\mathcal{J}}) = \mu_i(\mathbf{r}^*(E_{\mathcal{J}})) - D_i > 0,
\tag{6}

where \mathbf{r}^*(E_{\mathcal{J}}) are the resource concentrations at that equilibrium. Moreover, under this condition there exists \kappa > 0 such that \liminf_{t\to\infty} x_i(t) \ge \kappa for all i, uniformly for initial conditions in \operatorname{int}\mathbb{R}_+^{n_s+m}.

Proof.
Step 1: Carrying simplex.
For competitive systems, the dynamics (5) are of Kolmogorov form \dot{x}_i = x_i f_i(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{r}) with \partial f_i / \partial x_j \le 0 for j \neq i (competition for shared resources). By standard results (Hirsch, 1988; Smith, 1995), there exists a globally attracting invariant hypersurface \Sigma of codimension one—the carrying simplex—that contains all non‑trivial asymptotic dynamics. Uniform persistence of the full system is equivalent to the boundary of \Sigma being a repellor.

Step 2: Boundary dynamics and invasion criterion.
On the boundary where guilds in \mathcal{J} are absent, the system reduces to a lower‑dimensional competitive system. Its \omega-limit set consists of equilibria, periodic orbits, or chaotic attractors; but for three‑dimensional competitive systems, the only possible limit sets are equilibria (Smith, 1995). The general result relies on average Lyapunov functions. Define P(\mathbf{z}) = \prod_{i=1}^{n_s} x_i^{p_i} with positive weights p_i to be chosen. Then

\frac{\dot{P}}{P} = \sum_{i} p_i \big( \mu_i(\mathbf{r}) - D_i \big).
\tag{7}

If for every ergodic invariant measure \mu supported on the boundary, we have \int \sum_i p_i (\mu_i - D_i) \, d\mu > 0, then \partial\Sigma is a repellor and the system is permanent (Hofbauer, 1981). For systems whose boundary attractors are just equilibria, this reduces to checking (6) at every boundary equilibrium E_{\mathcal{J}}: there must exist a missing guild i with \mu_i(\mathbf{r}^*) > D_i, because that guild can invade. The weights p_i can be found by solving a linear programming problem if the number of boundary equilibria is finite (which is true under our monotonicity assumptions).

Step 3: Reduction to the R^* rule.
With Liebig kinetics, the invasion condition (6) becomes: for the invading guild i,

\min_{j \in \mathcal{R}_i} \frac{\mu_{\max,ij}\, r_j^*}{K_{ij} + r_j^*} > D_i
\iff r_j^* > \frac{K_{ij} D_i}{\mu_{\max,ij} - D_i} =: R_{ij}^*, \quad \forall j \in \mathcal{R}_i.

Thus, guild i can invade if the resource concentrations exceed its break‑even thresholds R_{ij}^* for all its limiting resources. The well‑known R^* rule of chemostat theory is recovered when each guild is limited by a single resource. Coexistence requires a trade‑off: each guild must have the lowest R^* for a different resource. The positive steady state \mathbf{z}^* encodes such a trade‑off.

Step 4: Uniformity.
Because the carrying simplex is compact and the invasion growth rates are uniformly positive, the limit inferior is bounded away from zero uniformly in the initial data; the constant \kappa can be chosen as the smallest value of \min_i x_i over the interior attractor on \Sigma. ∎

5.2 Stochastic permanence and extinction bounds

Now return to the full hybrid SDE (1). We assume the noise intensity is small, \|\boldsymbol{\sigma}_\theta\| \le \sigma_0, and that the Markov chain \theta(t) is ergodic with stationary distribution \pi = (\pi_1,\dots,\pi_K).

Theorem 2′ (Stochastic permanence).
If the deterministic skeleton satisfies the uniform persistence condition of Theorem 2, and if for every boundary equilibrium E_{\mathcal{J}} we have the averaged invasion growth rate

\sum_{k=1}^K \pi_k \, \lambda_i^{(k)}(E_{\mathcal{J}}) > 0
\tag{8}

(where \lambda_i^{(k)} denotes the invasion growth rate in regime k), then for sufficiently small \sigma_0 the process \mathbf{Z}(t) = (\mathbf{x}(t),\mathbf{r}(t)) is stochastically permanent: there exists a unique stationary distribution supported on the interior of \mathbb{R}_+^{n_s+m}, and for any initial interior condition the process is positive recurrent. Moreover, the extinction probability within a mission time T satisfies

\mathbb{P}\big( \min_{i} \inf_{[0,T]} x_i(t) < \varepsilon \big) \le C(T)\, \exp\!\Big( -\frac{I_\varepsilon}{\sigma_0^2} \Big),
\tag{9}

where I_\varepsilon is the Freidlin–Wentzell quasipotential barrier from the interior attractor to the boundary layer \{x_i \le \varepsilon\}.

Proof (sketch).
Consider the Lyapunov function V(\mathbf{z}) = -\sum_i p_i \ln x_i, with the weights p_i from the deterministic proof. Applying the infinitesimal generator \mathcal{L} of the hybrid SDE, one obtains

\mathcal{L} V = -\sum_i p_i (\mu_i(\mathbf{r}) - D_i) + \tfrac{1}{2} \operatorname{tr}\!\big( \boldsymbol{\sigma}_{\theta}^{\!\top} \operatorname{Hess} V \boldsymbol{\sigma}_{\theta} \big).

Near the boundary, the first term dominates and is negative if the deterministic invasion condition holds uniformly across all regimes and the noise is weak. By a standard Has’minskii criterion, the process is positive recurrent and possesses a stationary distribution on the interior. The extinction bound (9) follows from the large‑deviation theory for hybrid diffusions (Budhiraja & Dupuis, 2000): the most probable exit path from the basin of attraction crosses the boundary at the point of minimal quasipotential I_\varepsilon, yielding the exponential bound. ∎

