6. The MVE as a Mixed‑Integer Nonlinear Program

The MVE for a given planetary environment is defined as the Pareto‑minimal set with respect to the number of biological guilds and abiotic processors. We formulate the following MINLP:

\begin{aligned}
\min_{\mathbf{u},\, \mathbf{y},\, \mathbf{x}^*,\, \mathbf{f}^*} \quad & \sum_{i=1}^{n_s} u_i + \sum_{j=1}^{n_Q} y_j \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & \mathbf{N}(\mathbf{y})\,\mathbf{f}^* = \mathbf{b} \quad \text{(stoichiometry)} \\
& \mathbf{A} \ln \mathbf{r}^* < \mathbf{c} \quad \text{(thermodynamics)} \\
& \text{deterministic persistence conditions (Theorem 2)} \\
& \text{elemental conservation} \\
& u_i \in \{0,1\},\; y_j \in \{0,1\},\; \mathbf{x}^* > 0,\; \mathbf{r}^* > 0.
\end{aligned}

The bilinear terms \mathbf{N}(\mathbf{y})\,\mathbf{f}^* are handled by big‑M constraints, transforming them into linear inequalities. The thermodynamic constraints are linear in the log‑resources. The persistence constraints are algebraic: for each candidate boundary equilibrium of the selected guild set, the invasion eigenvalue (6) must be positive. In practice, these will be added as lazy cuts within a branch‑and‑bound solver: when a candidate integer solution is found, we compute all boundary equilibria numerically and check the invasion conditions; if violated, a cut is added that forbids the current guild set or forces the inclusion of an invader.

Because the number of possible guilds is modest (on the order of 10–20), the MINLP is tractable with modern algorithms.

