Appendix A – Mathematical Preliminaries

A.1 Notation

· \mathbb{R}_+ – non‑negative reals; \mathbb{R}_{>0} – positive reals.
· \mathbb{R}^n_+ – non‑negative orthant.
· For a vector \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{v}>0 means all components positive; \mathbf{v}\ge 0 component‑wise.
· |\cdot| – Euclidean norm.
· \mathcal{C}^k – k‑times continuously differentiable.
· \mathcal{M} = \{1,\dots,K\} – set of environmental regimes.

A.2 Dynamical systems and persistence

Consider an autonomous ODE \dot{\mathbf{z}} = \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{z}) on a positively invariant set \Omega \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n_+.

Definition A.1 (Uniform persistence). The system is uniformly persistent with respect to a set of essential components \mathcal{I} \subseteq \{1,\dots,n\} if there exists \eta > 0 such that for all initial conditions with z_i(0)>0 for i \in \mathcal{I},

\liminf_{t\to\infty} z_i(t) \ge \eta \qquad \forall i \in \mathcal{I}.

Theorem A.2 (Average Lyapunov function; Hofbauer 1981). Suppose \Omega is compact, forward invariant, and \mathbf{F} \in \mathcal{C}^1. If there exists a function V: \Omega \to \mathbb{R}, \mathcal{C}^1 on the interior and continuous on \Omega, such that

1. V(\mathbf{z}) \to \infty as \mathbf{z} approaches the boundary \partial\Omega (i.e., a proper barrier function),
2. there exists a continuous function \psi: \Omega \to \mathbb{R} with \dot{V}(\mathbf{z}) \ge \psi(\mathbf{z}) V(\mathbf{z}) on the interior, and for every boundary limit point \mathbf{z}^* of an interior trajectory, \limsup_{t\to\infty} \frac{1}{t}\int_0^t \psi(\mathbf{z}(s))\,ds > 0,

then the system is uniformly persistent. A common choice is V(\mathbf{z}) = -\sum_{i\in\mathcal{I}} \ln z_i, giving \dot{V} = -\sum_{i\in\mathcal{I}} \dot{z}_i/z_i. Uniform persistence follows if the time average of the per‑capita growth rates is positive along the boundary.

Carrying simplex. For competitive Kolmogorov systems \dot{x}_i = x_i f_i(\mathbf{x}) with \partial f_i/\partial x_j \le 0 (i \neq j), a globally attracting invariant hypersurface of codimension one (the carrying simplex) exists. Uniform persistence is then equivalent to the boundary of the simplex being a repellor (Hirsch 1988, Smith 1995).

A.3 Liebig–Monod kinetics

For guild i, the specific growth rate is given by the minimum law:

\mu_i(\mathbf{r}) = \min_{j \in \mathcal{R}_i} \mu_{ij}(r_j), \qquad \mu_{ij}(r) = \frac{\mu_{\max,ij}\, r}{K_{ij} + r},

where \mathcal{R}_i is the set of limiting resources. The break‑even concentration for resource j is R_{ij}^* = \dfrac{K_{ij} D_i}{\mu_{\max,ij} - D_i}, with D_i the mortality/dilution rate. Guild i can invade if r_j > R_{ij}^* for all j \in \mathcal{R}_i. A regularised smooth form \mu_i(\mathbf{r}) = \big(\sum_j \mu_{ij}(r_j)^{-1}\big)^{-1} may be used.

A.4 Chemical Reaction Network Theory (CRNT)

A reaction network \mathcal{N} = (\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{R}) consists of complexes \mathcal{C} and reactions \mathcal{R}. The stoichiometric matrix \mathbf{N} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times r} maps reaction fluxes to species changes. The deficiency is \delta = |\mathcal{C}| - \ell - s, where \ell is the number of linkage classes and s = \operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{N}). A network is weakly reversible if every reaction is part of a directed cycle.

Theorem A.3 (Deficiency Zero Theorem; Feinberg 1987). If a mass‑action network is weakly reversible and has deficiency zero, then each stoichiometric compatibility class \mathcal{P} = (\mathbf{c} + \operatorname{Im}\mathbf{N}) \cap \mathbb{R}_{>0}^n contains precisely one positive equilibrium, which is complex‑balanced and asymptotically stable regardless of the rate constants.

A.5 Convex cones and Farkas’ lemma

Let \mathbf{A} \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n}. The cone generated by the columns of \mathbf{A} is \operatorname{cone}(\mathbf{A}) = \{\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x} : \mathbf{x} \ge 0\}. Farkas’ lemma states: \mathbf{b} \in \operatorname{cone}(\mathbf{A}) iff \forall \mathbf{y} with \mathbf{y}^{\!\top}\mathbf{A} \ge 0, we have \mathbf{y}^{\!\top}\mathbf{b} \ge 0.

A.6 Thermodynamic linearisation

For reaction k, the Gibbs free energy change is \Delta G_k(\mathbf{r}) = \Delta G_k^\circ + RT \sum_j \nu_{kj} \ln r_j, where \nu_{kj} is the net consumption of resource j. The condition \Delta G_k < 0 is equivalent to \boldsymbol{\nu}_k^{\!\top} \mathbf{u} < -\Delta G_k^\circ/(RT) = c_k, with \mathbf{u} = \ln \mathbf{r}. The set \mathcal{T} = \{\mathbf{u} \in \mathbb{R}^m : \mathbf{A}\mathbf{u} < \mathbf{c}\} is a convex open polyhedron.

A.7 Stochastic differential equations with regime switching

On a filtered probability space (\Omega,\mathcal{F},\{\mathcal{F}_t\},\mathbb{P}), let \mathbf{W} be an m-dimensional Wiener process and \theta(t) a continuous‑time Markov chain on \mathcal{M} with generator Q = (q_{ij}). The hybrid SDE is

d\mathbf{X}(t) = \mathbf{f}_{\theta(t)}(\mathbf{X}(t))\,dt + \boldsymbol{\sigma}_{\theta(t)}(\mathbf{X}(t))\,d\mathbf{W}(t),

with coefficients locally Lipschitz and linear growth. The infinitesimal generator is

\mathcal{L} V(\mathbf{x},k) = \nabla V \!\cdot\! \mathbf{f}_k + \frac{1}{2}\operatorname{tr}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_k^{\!\top} \operatorname{Hess} V \boldsymbol{\sigma}_k) + \sum_{\ell \neq k} q_{k\ell} \big( V(\mathbf{x},\ell) - V(\mathbf{x},k) \big).

Large deviations (Freidlin–Wentzell). For a fixed regime, the quasipotential from an attractor \mathbf{x}^* to a point \mathbf{y} is V(\mathbf{y}) = \inf_{T>0,\; \varphi(0)=\mathbf{x}^*,\;\varphi(T)=\mathbf{y}} I(\varphi), with rate function I(\varphi) = \frac{1}{2}\int_0^T \|\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{-1}(\dot{\varphi} - \mathbf{f}(\varphi))\|^2 dt. The first exit time \tau from a domain G satisfies \mathbb{P}(\tau \le T) \asymp \exp(-\inf_{\partial G} V / \epsilon) for small noise intensity \epsilon.

