Appendix B – Proof of Theorem 1 (Stoichiometric–Thermodynamic Feasibility)

Theorem 1. Consider an ecosystem network with stoichiometric matrix \mathbf{N} \in \mathbb{R}^{(n_s+m) \times n_r}, net external demand \mathbf{b} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_s+m}, and rate functions f_k(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{r}) that are continuous, strictly positive on the interior, and strictly monotone in each resource argument. Define

\mathcal{F} = \{\mathbf{f} \ge 0 : \mathbf{N}\mathbf{f} = \mathbf{b}\}, \qquad
\mathcal{T} = \{\mathbf{u} \in \mathbb{R}^m : \mathbf{A}\mathbf{u} < \mathbf{c}\},

where \mathbf{A} and \mathbf{c} encode the linearised thermodynamic constraints \Delta G_k(\mathbf{r}) < 0 via \mathbf{u} = \ln \mathbf{r}. Assume the closed subsystem (\mathbf{b}=0) forms a weakly reversible network with deficiency \delta = 0. Then a strictly positive steady state (\mathbf{x}^*,\mathbf{r}^*) > 0 with thermodynamically feasible fluxes exists iff

\text{(i)}\;\; \mathbf{b} \in \operatorname{cone}(\mathbf{N}), \qquad
\text{(ii)}\;\; \mathcal{T} \neq \emptyset \;\text{and there exists }\mathbf{f} \in \mathcal{F}\text{ whose corresponding log‑resource vector lies in }\mathcal{T}.

Moreover, the steady state is unique in its stoichiometric compatibility class and locally asymptotically stable.

Proof.

1. Necessity.
If (\mathbf{x}^*,\mathbf{r}^*) is a positive steady state, then \mathbf{f}^* = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}^*,\mathbf{r}^*) \ge 0 satisfies \mathbf{N}\mathbf{f}^* = \mathbf{b}; hence \mathbf{b} \in \operatorname{cone}(\mathbf{N}). Thermodynamic feasibility gives \Delta G_k(\mathbf{r}^*) < 0 for all active fluxes, i.e., \mathbf{u}^* = \ln \mathbf{r}^* \in \mathcal{T}. Thus (i) and (ii) hold.

2. Sufficiency – construction of a positive steady state.

2.1. Flux cone feasibility.
Condition (i) guarantees \mathcal{F} \neq \emptyset. By Farkas’ lemma, an equivalent certificate is that for all \mathbf{y} with \mathbf{y}^{\!\top}\mathbf{N} \ge 0, we have \mathbf{y}^{\!\top}\mathbf{b} \ge 0. Since \mathbf{b} is a net demand, this condition is satisfied for any well‑posed closed ecosystem.

2.2. Thermodynamic feasibility.
The set \mathcal{T} is an open convex polyhedron. Condition (ii) states that the projection of \mathcal{F} onto the log‑resource space (through the rate laws) intersects \mathcal{T}. Because the rate laws f_k are strictly monotone in \mathbf{r}, the mapping \mathbf{f} \mapsto \mathbf{u} (via solving \mathbf{f} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{r}) for \mathbf{r} given a suitable \mathbf{x}) is continuous and invertible on the interior. Hence we can pick a flux vector \mathbf{f}^\circ \in \mathcal{F} and a corresponding log‑resource vector \mathbf{u}^\circ \in \mathcal{T}.

2.3. Closed subsystem and CRNT.
Set \mathbf{b} = 0 and consider only the internal reactions. The network is weakly reversible and deficiency zero. By the Deficiency Zero Theorem (Theorem A.3), for any positive rate constants (which we may take as the values of f_k at the desired steady state) there exists a unique positive equilibrium \mathbf{z}_0^* in each stoichiometric compatibility class. This equilibrium is complex‑balanced and globally asymptotically stable with Lyapunov function V(\mathbf{z}) = \sum_i z_i(\ln z_i - \ln z_{0i}^* - 1).

2.4. Perturbation by the external flux.
The full system with external demand \mathbf{b} can be written as \dot{\mathbf{z}} = \mathbf{N} \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{z}) - \mathbf{b}. We treat the demand as a small perturbation to the closed system. Consider the family of steady‑state equations

\mathbf{N} \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{z}) = \epsilon \mathbf{b}, \qquad \epsilon \in [0,1].

At \epsilon = 0, a positive steady state \mathbf{z}_0^* exists and is hyperbolic (the Jacobian is Hurwitz). By the implicit function theorem, for sufficiently small \epsilon there exists a unique smooth branch \mathbf{z}^*(\epsilon) > 0 satisfying the perturbed equation. The theorem’s conditions ensure that this branch can be extended to \epsilon = 1 using a global fixed‑point argument.

2.5. Fixed‑point argument for \epsilon = 1.
Define a map \Phi : \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{U} on a compact, convex set \mathcal{U} \subset \mathbb{R}_{>0}^m as follows. Take \mathbf{u} = \ln \mathbf{r}. Given \mathbf{u}, the resources \mathbf{r} = e^{\mathbf{u}} are determined. Using the chosen flux vector \mathbf{f}^\circ \in \mathcal{F} \cap \mathcal{T}, set the biomass levels to \mathbf{x} = \operatorname{diag}(\mu_i(\mathbf{r}))^{-1} \mathbf{f}^\circ (component‑wise, if guilds are independent). The rate laws then satisfy f_k(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{r}) = f_k^\circ. The resources must satisfy the steady‑state balance, which we write as \dot{\mathbf{r}} = 0. Because the fluxes and the stoichiometry are fixed, the resource equilibrium condition becomes a set of algebraic equations \mathbf{N}_{\mathbf{r}} \mathbf{f}^\circ = \mathbf{b}_{\mathbf{r}} (where \mathbf{N}_{\mathbf{r}} are the rows corresponding to resources). These equations are linear in the fluxes and already satisfied by construction of \mathcal{F}. Hence any \mathbf{u} that yields the correct \mathbf{x} via the monotone mapping will be a fixed point. The map \Phi that updates \mathbf{u} based on the resource balance is continuous and maps a compact convex set (defined by the intersection of the thermodynamic cone and the flux‑cone projection) into itself. By Brouwer’s fixed‑point theorem, there exists \mathbf{u}^* with \Phi(\mathbf{u}^*) = \mathbf{u}^*, giving a positive steady state.

2.6. Uniqueness and stability.
Because the closed subsystem has deficiency zero, the perturbed system inherits a unique equilibrium in each compatibility class when \|\mathbf{b}\| is not too large; the global extension follows from the monotonicity of the rate laws. Local asymptotic stability is proved by linearising: the Jacobian \mathbf{J} is a perturbation of the closed system’s Jacobian, which is Hurwitz. A continuity argument guarantees that the eigenvalues remain in the left half‑plane for \mathbf{b} up to the demanded value. ∎

