Appendix C – Proof of Theorem 2 (Uniform Persistence and Stochastic Permanence)

Theorem 2 (Deterministic). Consider the closed ecosystem (\mathbf{b}=0) with Liebig–Monod kinetics (2) and dissipative dynamics. Assume a strictly positive steady state exists (Theorem 1). For any boundary equilibrium E_{\mathcal{J}} (where the set of guilds \mathcal{J} \subsetneq \{1,\dots,n_s\} is extinct) let the invasion growth rate of a missing guild i \notin \mathcal{J} be

\lambda_i(E_{\mathcal{J}}) = \mu_i(\mathbf{r}^*(E_{\mathcal{J}})) - D_i .

The system is uniformly persistent iff for every boundary equilibrium E_{\mathcal{J}} there exists i \notin \mathcal{J} with \lambda_i(E_{\mathcal{J}}) > 0. In that case, there exists \kappa > 0 such that \liminf_{t\to\infty} x_i(t) \ge \kappa for all i.

Proof.

Step 1: Carrying simplex.
The system is of Kolmogorov form with \partial \dot{x}_i/\partial x_j \le 0 for j \neq i. Hence it admits a carrying simplex \Sigma, a globally attracting invariant hypersurface homeomorphic to the probability simplex. The boundary of \Sigma consists of faces where at least one species is extinct. Uniform persistence holds iff the boundary is a repellor on \Sigma.

Step 2: Average Lyapunov function.
Choose a vector of positive weights \mathbf{p} \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^{n_s} and define P(\mathbf{x}) = \prod_{i=1}^{n_s} x_i^{p_i}. Then

\frac{\dot{P}}{P} = \sum_{i} p_i (\mu_i(\mathbf{r}) - D_i).

According to Theorem A.2, if there exists \mathbf{p} such that for every ergodic invariant measure supported on the boundary, the time average of \sum_i p_i(\mu_i - D_i) is strictly positive, then the boundary is a repellor and the system is uniformly persistent. For competitive three‑dimensional systems the boundary attractors are equilibria (Smith 1995); more generally, we assume a finite number of boundary equilibria E_{\mathcal{J}}. The condition thus reduces to demanding that at each E_{\mathcal{J}}, there exists a missing guild i \notin \mathcal{J} with \mu_i(\mathbf{r}^*) > D_i. Indeed, if this holds, we can select weights p_i that concentrate on the potential invaders; the linear programming problem for \mathbf{p} has a feasible solution because the invasion growth rates are linearly independent from the equilibrium constraints.

Step 3: Invasion and the R^* rule.
Using Liebig–Monod kinetics, the inequality \mu_i(\mathbf{r}^*) > D_i is equivalent to \min_{j \in \mathcal{R}_i} \frac{\mu_{\max,ij} r_j^*}{K_{ij} + r_j^*} > D_i, which in turn is equivalent to r_j^* > R_{ij}^* for all limiting resources j \in \mathcal{R}_i. Thus, each boundary equilibrium must leave at least one guild with resource concentrations exceeding its break‑even thresholds. This is a multispecies extension of the R^* rule. The existence of a positive steady state ensures that the necessary trade‑offs among guilds are present.

Step 4: Uniformity.
Because the carrying simplex is compact and the minimum invasion rate over all boundary equilibria is strictly positive, the limit inferior is bounded away from zero uniformly in the initial conditions. One may take \kappa = \min_{\text{attractor}} \min_i x_i. ∎

Theorem 2′ (Stochastic permanence). Under the conditions of Theorem 2, consider the hybrid SDE (1) with small noise \|\boldsymbol{\sigma}_\theta\| \le \sigma_0 and ergodic Markov chain \theta(t) with stationary distribution \pi. If for every boundary equilibrium E_{\mathcal{J}},

\sum_{k=1}^K \pi_k \, \lambda_i^{(k)}(E_{\mathcal{J}}) > 0 \quad \text{for some } i \notin \mathcal{J},

then for sufficiently small \sigma_0 the process is stochastically permanent: it has a unique stationary distribution on the interior and is positive recurrent. Moreover,

\mathbb{P}\Big( \min_i \inf_{[0,T]} x_i(t) < \varepsilon \Big) \le C(T) \exp\!\Big( -\frac{I_\varepsilon}{\sigma_0^2} \Big),

where I_\varepsilon is the quasipotential barrier to the boundary layer.

Proof (sketch).
Adopt the Lyapunov function V(\mathbf{x}) = -\sum_i p_i \ln x_i from the deterministic proof. The generator for the hybrid SDE satisfies

\mathcal{L} V = -\sum_i p_i (\mu_i - D_i) + \frac{1}{2}\operatorname{tr}\big( \boldsymbol{\sigma}_\theta^{\!\top} \operatorname{Hess} V \boldsymbol{\sigma}_\theta \big).

The Hessian of -\ln x_i contributes terms proportional to 1/x_i^2, which are bounded below near the boundary. Under the averaged invasion condition, the drift term dominates and \mathcal{L} V is negative outside a compact set in the interior. By a standard criterion for positive recurrence (Has’minskii), the process admits a unique stationary distribution. The large deviation bound follows from Freidlin–Wentzell theory for hybrid processes: exit from the safe set is governed by the quasipotential I_\varepsilon, yielding the exponential estimate. ∎

