Appendix D – MINLP Formulation and Solution Strategy

The MVE problem is

\begin{aligned}
\min_{\mathbf{u},\, \mathbf{y},\, \mathbf{x}^*,\, \mathbf{f}^*} \quad & \sum_{i=1}^{n_s} u_i + \sum_{j=1}^{n_Q} y_j \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & \mathbf{N}(\mathbf{y})\mathbf{f}^* = \mathbf{b}, \quad \mathbf{A} \ln \mathbf{r}^* < \mathbf{c}, \\
& \mathbf{x}^* = \operatorname{diag}(\boldsymbol{\mu}(\mathbf{r}^*))^{-1} \mathbf{f}^*_{\text{guilds}}, \quad \mathbf{f}^* \ge 0,\\
& \text{persistence cuts (Theorem 2)},\\
& u_i, y_j \in \{0,1\},\; \mathbf{x}^* > 0,\; \mathbf{r}^* > 0.
\end{aligned}

Linearisation of bilinear terms.
The term \mathbf{N}(\mathbf{y})\mathbf{f}^* contains products of binaries and continuous fluxes. For each flux f_k and processor y_j that multiplies it, we introduce an auxiliary variable z_{kj} = y_j f_k and enforce

0 \le z_{kj} \le M y_j, \qquad f_k - M(1-y_j) \le z_{kj} \le f_k,

where M is a sufficiently large constant (big‑M formulation). Similarly, the biomass equation x_i = f_i / \mu_i(\mathbf{r}) is non‑convex. We apply McCormick envelopes or piecewise‑linear approximations for \mu_i(\mathbf{r}) over a bounded domain.

Persistence cuts.
After a candidate integer solution (\mathbf{u}^*,\mathbf{y}^*) is obtained, we compute all boundary equilibria of the corresponding deterministic system using numerical continuation (or, for small problems, algebraic solution). For each boundary equilibrium E_{\mathcal{J}}, we evaluate the invasion eigenvalues \lambda_i. If there exists an equilibrium where all missing guilds have \lambda_i \le 0, the candidate is not persistent. We then add a “no‑good” cut that forbids the current set of guilds unless at least one additional guild is included or the resource supply is modified. For instance:

\sum_{i \notin \mathcal{J}} u_i \ge 1,

which forces the inclusion of a potential invader. The procedure is iterated within the branch‑and‑bound tree.

Algorithm outline.

1. Construct catalogue of candidate guilds and processors, with thermodynamic and kinetic parameters.
2. Pre‑check: for each subset of guilds (or dynamically), verify (i) stoichiometric feasibility \mathbf{b} \in \operatorname{cone}(\mathbf{N}) and (ii) thermodynamic feasibility via LP.
3. Solve the MINLP using Juniper (branch‑and‑bound with Ipopt for NLP subproblems).
4. When an integer solution is found, call the persistence checker:
   · Compute all boundary equilibria by setting extinct guilds to zero and solving the reduced system.
   · Evaluate invasion eigenvalues.
   · If failure, add cut and continue branching.
5. Return the configuration with the smallest total number of components that satisfies all constraints.

