Appendix E – Large Deviation Bound for Extinction (Freidlin–Wentzell)

Consider a fixed regime SDE d\mathbf{X} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{X}) dt + \sqrt{\epsilon}\,\boldsymbol{\sigma}(\mathbf{X}) d\mathbf{W} with small noise \epsilon. The action functional on [0,T] is

S_T(\varphi) = \frac{1}{2} \int_0^T \big\| \boldsymbol{\sigma}(\varphi_t)^{-1} (\dot{\varphi}_t - \mathbf{f}(\varphi_t)) \big\|^2 dt.

The quasipotential from an asymptotically stable equilibrium \mathbf{x}^* to a point \mathbf{y} is

V(\mathbf{y}) = \inf_{T>0,\; \varphi(0)=\mathbf{x}^*,\; \varphi(T)=\mathbf{y}} S_T(\varphi).

Let G = \{\mathbf{x} : x_i > \varepsilon \; \forall i\} be the safe set. The first exit time \tau_\varepsilon = \inf\{t \ge 0 : \mathbf{X}(t) \notin G\} satisfies the large deviation estimate

\lim_{\epsilon \to 0} \epsilon \ln \mathbb{P}(\tau_\varepsilon \le T) = - \inf_{\mathbf{y} \in \partial G} V(\mathbf{y}).

For the hybrid case, the quasipotential incorporates the cost of regime switches: the rate function for switching from regime i to j is -\ln q_{ij} on the log scale. The barrier I_\varepsilon is the minimum over all boundary points and all possible regime sequences. Theorem 2′ follows by applying this bound with \epsilon = \sigma_0^2.

Thus, the extinction probability constraint in the MVE can be approximated by requiring \inf_{\partial G} V \ge -\sigma_0^2 \ln(\delta/C(T)), which is a deterministic inequality linking the steady state and noise level.